But what if a class attr named a exists (shadow case)? can't be: slot unshadowed means no class attr in MRO... but a class attr deeper than the slot (slot declared in derived class, class var in base): `class B: a=3; class S(B): __slots__=("a",)` — S's slot a; lookup("a") finds B.a=3... In CPython, member descriptor in S shadows B.a=3 for instance reads; reading after del s.a → member descriptor with no value → "no attribute"? Let me not test that edge; our get_attr: attrs removed → cls.lookup finds 3 → returns 3! Mismatch. But that edge (slot shadowing base class var) is obscure. CPython: getattr finds S's member descriptor first (data descriptor) → unset → "no attribute 'a'". Our runtime would return 3. Pre-existing modeling limitation (we don't create member descriptors). Out of scope.

